the register's ceiling 2^62; the exact pair's product refuses every star (2^87); the grain form's flow product is 2^30 and then today's push 2^34.
the bias of the quantised speed is below 1 / G = 2.44e-04 Links per interval, downward in |v|; the weight's bias below T / (G N) = 55 / 131072 = 4.20e-04 of the rate on a heading.

==============================================================================
2. Bit-identity at rest and the bounds of the grain form
==============================================================================
at p = 0: w = 0, the pair is (G N_d, G N_d) = 1 exactly, by_clock(age, |V| x G N_d, G N_d) = |V|: bit-identical to today's flow, so to today's push, for every fixed body and every free body at rest.
the numerator bound on any direction of the table: G (N_d + T_d) <= 67108864 = 2^26.0; a flow of amount A on one direction: |V_d| x num <= A x Q x 2^26.0 = A x 2^32.0: fits for A up to 2^30 per direction per interval.
the load-time bound: none new beyond today's (the weighted flow is at most (1 + T_d / N_d) x the flow, at most 4.75 x on the heading and 3 x |D|^2 / a^2 ... on a fan direction; the static budget of the columns takes the flow's largest value times that factor).

==============================================================================
3. The oblique weight: per axis against the flux, a body moving on x
==============================================================================
for a direction D = (a, b, c) with T_d = isqrt(3 |D|^2 Q^2): the message's velocity is (Q / T_d) D per axis (Links per interval),
  the per-axis form (as built): 1 - v_x / c_{d,x} = 1 - v_x T_d / (Q a);  the flux form: 1 - (v . c) / |c|^2 = 1 - v_x T_d a / (Q |D|^2)
   direction   T_d      c_x  per-axis @1/3  flux @1/3  per-axis @star  flux @star  ratio of the v-terms
   (1, 0, 0)   110   0.5818         0.4271     0.4271          0.5035      0.5035                     1
   (1, 1, 0)   156   0.4103         0.1875     0.5938          0.2958      0.6479                     2
   (1, 1, 1)   192   0.3333         0.0000     0.6667          0.1333      0.7111                     3
   (2, 1, 0)   247   0.5182         0.3568     0.4854          0.4425      0.5540                   5/4
   (3, 1, 0)   350   0.5486         0.3924     0.4531          0.4734      0.5260                  10/9
   (5, 1, 0)   565   0.5664         0.4115     0.4341          0.4899      0.5095                 26/25
   (1, 2, 0)   247   0.2591        -0.2865     0.7427         -0.1149      0.7770                     5
   (1, 3, 2)   414   0.1546        -1.1562     0.8460         -0.8688      0.8665                    14
the per-axis form equals the flux form on a heading (|D|^2 = a^2) and overstates the Doppler term by |D|^2 / a^2 on every other direction:
  x 2 on a face diagonal, x 3 on a cube diagonal, x 26 on (5, 1, 0) read by a body moving on y ... (the term with the small component).
the flux form in the grain's integers, one scalar per (row direction, body), applied to the row's whole label vector:
  f_d = (G Q |D|^2 - T_d sum_a s_a w_a D_a) / (G Q |D|^2), w_a = G |p_a| // D_a per axis, s_a = sign(p_a);
  the numerator bound G (Q |D|^2 + T_d S_1) <= 12884901888 = 2^33.6; a flow of amount A: A x Q x 2^33.6: fits for A up to 2^22.
